Wait: `syncExtentChip()` uses `qs('[data-map-cmd="extents"]')` which returns the *first* match — which is now the chip in the skip list. The second (hint link) also needs syncing. Let me make syncExtentChip handle all matches: `document.querySelectorAll('[data-map-cmd="extents"]')`.

Also the hint link inside the paragraph — the text should toggle too ("„Ausschnitte“" → "„Ausschnitte“ ausblenden"?). Keep the link label stable: `„Ausschnitte“` with aria-pressed; but then the label doesn't express the action... Actually a toggle link labelled “Ausschnitte” with aria-pressed is fine and the status line reports the state. But mixing: chip toggles text "Ausschnitte zeigen"/"Ausschnitte ausblenden", link stays "„Ausschnitte“". Slight inconsistency but acceptable — actually let me simplify: make the chip text stable-ish too? Hmm.

The harness needs a stable hook: `data-map-cmd="extents"` is stable. The chip text can toggle. For the inline link, keep static text `„Ausschnitte“` and use aria-pressed. But `aria-pressed` on a `<button>` styled as inline text — accessible name "„Ausschnitte“" + pressed state. OK.

Hmm, is including a second control in the hint paragraph a good idea? It's an inline button with underline styling — discoverable right in the instructions. I think yes: it directly connects the instruction to the control. But two controls for one state... The map skip-list chips already duplicate icon controls, so this is consistent with the app's style.

Now CSS for `.map-footprint` and `.app-map__hintlink`.

Let me now update syncExtentChip to handle both, and set text only on the chip (the first one, in the skip list). I'll use a data attribute for the label? Simplest:

```js
  function syncExtentChip() {
    const chips = document.querySelectorAll('[data-map-cmd="extents"]');
    const on = state.extents === '1';
    for (const chip of chips) {
