Sequence B (rawKeyDown + char + keyUp) activates the native button. So the shared `pressKey` lacks the `char` event for Enter — which means existing harness checks that press Enter (e.g. the place-search suggestion) work because they handle `keydown` in JS, not native activation.

For my check, use the explicit sequence via `cdp.send`. Let me implement a tiny local helper in my section:

```js
  await cdp.eval(`document.querySelector('.app-map__hintlink[data-map-cmd="extents"]').focus(); true`);
  // A native <button> is activated by the browser's default action, which needs
  // the full key sequence (rawKeyDown + char + keyUp) — the shared pressKey only
  // sends keyDown/keyUp and is enough for JS keydown handlers, not for this.
  const enter = { key: 'Enter', code: 'Enter', windowsVirtualKeyCode: 13, nativeVirtualKeyCode: 13 };
  await cdp.send('Input.dispatchKeyEvent', { type: 'rawKeyDown', ...enter });
  await cdp.send('Input.dispatchKeyEvent', { type: 'char', text: '\r', unmodifiedText: '\r', ...enter });
  await cdp.send('Input.dispatchKeyEvent', { type: 'keyUp', ...enter });
```

Hmm — alternatively I could improve the shared `pressKey` to send the char event for Enter, which would make the whole harness more faithful (and possibly fix latent behavior). But it risks changing other checks' behavior (e.g. one that presses Enter in a text input). I'll keep it local and note it in coordination for maps-3 (suggest improving the shared helper).

